{"id":114269,"title":"Implicit Occluders","url":"https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1411304/?q=marching cubes","final_url":"https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1411304/m2/1/high_res_d/15013912.pdf","year":null,"kind":"link","source":"os:digital.library.unt.edu","doi":null,"arxiv":null,"sha256":"71a949581d06dfd8ce558c4c79d84b62933616dc829e940824d632adfa785c1b","ctype":"application/pdf","text_src":"url","fetched_at":1793236067.001082,"text_chars":38591,"abstract":"In this paper we propose a novel visibility-culling technique for optimizing the computation and rendering of opaque isosurfaces. Given a continuous scalar field f (x) over a domain D and an isovalue w, our technique exploits the continuity of f to determine conservative visibility bounds implicitly, i.e., without the need for actually computing the isosurface f{sup -1}(w). We generate Implicit Occluders based on the change in sign of f *(x) = f (x)-w, from positive to negative (or vice versa) in the neighborhood of the isosurface. Consider, for example, the sign of f * along a ray r cast from the current viewpoint. The first change in sign of f * within D must contain an intersection of r with the isosurface. Any additional intersection of the isosurface with r is not visible. Implicit Occluders constitute a general concept that can be exploited algorithmically in different ways depending on the framework adopted for visibility computations. In this paper, we propose a simple from-point approach that exploits well-known hardware occlusion queries.","file":null,"marks":["unofficial"],"links":["https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1411304/m2/1/high_res_d/15013912.pdf","https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1411304/?q=marching cubes","https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1411304/?q=isosurface extraction","https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1411304/?q=isosurface ray tracing volume","https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc14113